{"conjecture": "Given a relational property test suite (pairs of inputs constrained by a relation R, with outputs constrained by a relation S), the shortest program that minimally satisfies all test pairs will generalize to satisfy the full input-output relation better than programs synthesized from independent input-output examples. The relational coupling acts as an implicit specification of intermediate invariants, so a search procedure that treats test pairs as inseparable constraints—rather than decomposing them into individual examples—will converge on correct implementations of operations like sorting or deduplication where pointwise examples are ambiguous. The falsifiable claim: on a benchmark of relations with nontrivial internal structure, relational-pair synthesis yields programs passing held-out property tests at twice the rate of equivalent-example-count pointwise synthesis."}
